Over days twenty-two to twenty-five, I learnt how to secure Cisco switches against common Layer 2 attacks by implementing various switch security features. The module focused on port security, VLAN attack mitigation, DHCP Snooping, Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI), Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) security, and practical switch hardening techniques. These lessons built upon the LAN Security Concepts module by teaching me how to configure security features that protect enterprise switching environments.

11.1 Implement Port Security
1. Secure Unused Ports

I learnt that unused switch ports should never be left active because they can provide attackers with unauthorised access to the network. I practised securing unused ports by:

Shutting down inactive interfaces.
Assigning unused ports to an unused VLAN.
Labelling ports for easier management.

This taught me that securing unused ports is one of the simplest yet most effective security measures.

2. Mitigate MAC Address Table Attacks

I learnt how Port Security protects switches against MAC address table flooding attacks by restricting which devices can connect to a switch port.

This reinforced the importance of preventing unauthorised devices from accessing the network.

3. Enable Port Security

I learnt how to enable Port Security on switch interfaces. I understood that Port Security:

Restricts network access to authorised devices.
Monitors connected MAC addresses.
Protects against unauthorised connections.

This introduced me to one of the most important switch security features.

4. Limit and Learn MAC Addresses

I learnt how switches can:

Manually configure trusted MAC addresses.
Dynamically learn secure MAC addresses.
Limit the number of MAC addresses allowed on each interface.

This helps prevent unauthorised devices from connecting to the network.

5. Port Security Aging

I learnt that Port Security aging removes secure MAC addresses after a configured period of inactivity. I understood that:

Aging allows network flexibility.
Devices can be replaced without manual intervention.
Secure MAC tables remain up to date.

This demonstrated how security can be maintained without reducing usability.

6. Port Security Violation Modes

I learnt the three Port Security violation modes:

Protect

Drops traffic from unauthorised devices while keeping the port active.

Restrict

Drops unauthorised traffic, records violations, and generates alerts.

Shutdown

Places the port into an error-disabled state when a violation occurs.

This helped me understand how administrators respond to security violations.

7. Ports in Error-Disabled State

I learnt that a switch automatically disables a port after certain security violations occur. I also learnt how administrators can recover ports after correcting the problem.

This improved my understanding of switch recovery procedures.

8. Verify Port Security

I learnt how to verify Port Security using Cisco IOS commands. I checked:

Secure MAC addresses
Port status
Violation counts
Security configuration

This strengthened my verification and troubleshooting skills.

9. Syntax Checker – Implement Port Security

I practised Port Security commands using a syntax checker, improving my confidence in configuring Cisco switches correctly.

10. Packet Tracer – Implement Port Security

I completed a Packet Tracer activity where I:

