Verified switch behaviour.

This practical exercise reinforced the concepts covered in the lesson.

11.2 Mitigate VLAN Attacks
11. VLAN Attacks Review

I reviewed VLAN attacks such as VLAN hopping and double-tagging, understanding how attackers attempt to bypass VLAN separation.

This reinforced the importance of secure VLAN configuration.

12. Steps to Mitigate VLAN Hopping Attacks

I learnt several techniques used to prevent VLAN hopping attacks, including:

Disabling Dynamic Trunking Protocol (DTP) on access ports.
Manually configuring trunk ports.
Assigning an unused native VLAN.
Disabling unused switch ports.

These practices improve VLAN security in enterprise networks.

13. Syntax Checker – Mitigate VLAN Hopping Attacks

I practised configuring VLAN security settings using a syntax checker, helping me become more familiar with Cisco IOS commands.

11.3 Mitigate DHCP Attacks
14. DHCP Attack Review

I reviewed DHCP attacks, including:

Rogue DHCP servers.
DHCP starvation attacks.

I understood how these attacks disrupt legitimate network services.

15. DHCP Snooping

I learnt that DHCP Snooping protects networks by:

Allowing DHCP messages only from trusted ports.
Blocking rogue DHCP servers.
Building a trusted DHCP binding database.

This introduced me to one of the most important Layer 2 security features.

16. Steps to Implement DHCP Snooping

I learnt the process of configuring DHCP Snooping by:

Enabling DHCP Snooping globally.
Specifying trusted interfaces.
Enabling DHCP Snooping for selected VLANs.
Verifying the DHCP binding database.

This gave me practical knowledge of implementing DHCP protection.

17. DHCP Snooping Configuration Example

I studied configuration examples showing how DHCP Snooping is deployed on Cisco switches and how it protects clients against malicious DHCP servers.

18. Syntax Checker – Mitigate DHCP Attacks

I practised DHCP Snooping configuration commands using a syntax checker to improve my configuration accuracy.

11.4 Mitigate ARP Attacks
19. Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI)

I learnt that Dynamic ARP Inspection validates ARP packets before they are forwarded by comparing them against trusted DHCP Snooping bindings.

I understood that DAI helps prevent:

ARP spoofing
ARP poisoning
Man-in-the-Middle attacks
20. DAI Implementation Guidelines

I learnt the recommended practices for implementing DAI, including:

Enabling DHCP Snooping first.
Configuring trusted interfaces.
Applying DAI only to required VLANs.

This demonstrated how multiple security features work together.

21. DAI Configuration Example

I studied practical examples showing how to configure Dynamic ARP Inspection on Cisco switches and verify its operation.

22. Syntax Checker – Mitigate ARP Attacks

I practised DAI configuration commands using a syntax checker, strengthening my Cisco IOS configuration skills.

11.5 Mitigate STP Attacks
23. PortFast and BPDU Guard

I learnt how PortFast and BPDU Guard improve switch security.

PortFast

Allows end devices to begin forwarding traffic immediately without waiting for STP convergence.

BPDU Guard

Protects the network by disabling ports that receive unexpected Bridge Protocol Data Units (BPDUs).

These features prevent accidental or malicious changes to the spanning tree topology.

24. Configure PortFast

I learnt how to configure PortFast on access ports connected to end devices, reducing connection delays while maintaining network stability.

25. Configure BPDU Guard

I learnt how to configure BPDU Guard to automatically disable ports receiving unauthorised BPDUs, protecting the network against rogue switches.

26. Syntax Checker – Mitigate STP Attacks

I practised configuring PortFast and BPDU Guard using a syntax checker to improve my command-line proficiency.

